00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
the british government has released a  raft of policies today all aimed at  hitting ambitious carbon reduction  targets and demonstrating leadership  ahead of the crucial u.n climate summit  cop 26. so what's been announced well  the focus is on those sectors most  responsible for emissions that means  decarbonizing the way we heat our homes  so there's a 450 million pound fund to  help people switch from gas boilers to  low carbon alternatives like electric  heat pumps transport is also a big  emitter so the government will invest  620 million pounds in grants for  electric vehicles and street charging  points alongside other investments in  hydrogen and aviation fuel technologies  and of course all this electricity we're  going to be using has to be clean or  there's no point so the uk has now  committed to delivering exactly this by  2035 that means more wind and solar  power and an attempt to approve at least  one more large-scale nuclear power plant  in the coming years  the government says that its net zero  strategy will support 440 000 jobs by  2030 and that tens of billions of pounds  in private and public money has been  mobilized to pay for this green  industrial revolution although the  treasury has said that additional taxes  may be needed if there's a shortfall so  what next well all of these  announcements have been timed to help  boost the uk's position as a climate  leader ahead of the cop 26 meeting in  glasgow which is now just days away the  prime minister says that the uk strategy  sets an example to other nations to  raise their own net zero ambitions  but i've talked to senior members of the  chinese government who say that won't be  the case  china is the world's largest polluter  and has already committed to peak carbon  emissions before 2030 and net zero by  2060.  we do not need the actions taken by the  uk to encourage us  china also set up a very clear roadmap  together with a clear time frame  policies measures and actual feasible  actions to support its double  targets  we need  to have the biggest drop  biggest cut on the carbon intensity  compared with all the other countries  this is the best possible efforts we can  make given the current development stage  and the capacity of china  we simply hope all the developed  countries can do the same by honoring uh  their words with actual actions  the stage is now set for a difficult  climate summit if china continues to be  unwilling to make further concessions on  carbon reduction or on the domestic  phase-out of coal
 

